\title{Delayed Generalization in a Width-One ReLU Network\\and Its Saddle-Node Transition Model}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 3, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a minimal exact model of grokking-like delayed generalization and pair it with the canonical saddle-node normal form. The learning model is a scalar, width-one, two-layer rectified linear unit network whose time-indexed output is $G_d(t)=\max\{t-d,0\}$ for a prescribed delay $d$. Generalization is strict positivity of this scalar test score. We prove that the score is identically zero for $t\le d$ and strictly positive for $t>d$. We then classify every real equilibrium of $F_\mu(x)=\mu-x^2$: none for $\mu<0$, the unique degenerate equilibrium $x=0$ at $\mu=0$, and exactly two branches $x=\pm\sqrt\mu$ for $\mu>0$. The combined result is a self-contained threshold-and-bifurcation model. The construction isolates a mechanism rather than asserting universality: the trajectory is prescribed, and the bifurcation parameter is paired with, rather than derived from, a training loss. We also give exact algorithms, applications, limitations, and directions required for a dynamical theory of grokking in trained networks.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Grokking describes a learning pattern in which useful behavior on unseen data appears substantially later than successful fitting of observed data. Its striking feature is temporal separation: a system may display a long plateau in a generalization metric before crossing into rapid improvement. This raises distinct mathematical questions. What elementary mechanism can produce a perfectly delayed observable? In what sense is the change a phase transition? How might generalization be related to a qualitative change in an underlying dynamical system?

We answer the first two questions in the smallest setting where both are exact. The network has one scalar input, one rectified hidden unit, and one scalar output. Time serves as input, and a hidden bias places the activation threshold at any chosen delay $d$. Although the preactivation evolves continuously before the threshold, the visible score remains exactly zero. This separates latent motion from observable progress.

The phase-transition component is the saddle-node field $F_\mu(x)=\mu-x^2$. Its equilibrium set is empty for negative parameter, has one degenerate point at criticality, and has two square-root branches for positive parameter. The two constructions are paired rather than identified: time and bifurcation parameter play different roles, as do network score and dynamical state. The result therefore supplies a baseline against which mechanistic theories can be measured, rather than claiming that an optimizer generates this trajectory.

\section{Network model}
\begin{definition}[Rectified linear unit]
The rectified linear unit is
\[
\ReLU(z)=\max\{z,0\}=
\begin{cases}0,&z\le 0,\\z,&z>0.\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Scalar width-one two-layer network]
For real input weight $w$, hidden bias $b$, output weight $v$, output bias $c$, and input $x$, set
\[
N_{w,b,v,c}(x)=v\max\{wx+b,0\}+c.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Delayed score and generalization]
Fix a real delay $d$ and select $w=v=1$, $b=-d$, and $c=0$. With time $t$ as input, define
\[
G_d(t)=N_{1,-d,1,0}(t)=\max\{t-d,0\}.
\]
The network \emph{generalizes at time $t$} when $G_d(t)>0$.
\end{definition}

This criterion should be interpreted as positivity of a scalar test score or margin. It is narrower than population risk or accuracy on a test set unless a separate model identifies the score with one of those quantities.

\begin{lemma}[Pre-threshold vanishing]
For all $d,t\in\mathbb R$, if $t\le d$, then $G_d(t)=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The hypothesis implies $t-d\le0$. Hence $\max\{t-d,0\}=0$, which is $G_d(t)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Post-threshold positivity]
For all $d,t\in\mathbb R$, if $d<t$, then $G_d(t)=t-d>0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The hypothesis gives $t-d>0$, so the maximum selects $t-d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Delayed Generalization Theorem]
For every prescribed $d\in\mathbb R$, the score $G_d(t)=\max\{t-d,0\}$ fails the strict-positivity criterion at every $t\le d$ and satisfies it at every $t>d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Pre-threshold vanishing rules out positivity through $d$, while post-threshold positivity proves the criterion after $d$. These regimes exhaust $\mathbb R$.
\end{proof}

The latent preactivation $a_d(t)=t-d$ changes at constant rate even during the observed plateau. The gate collapses every nonpositive latent value to zero. Thus a flat observable need not imply static latent variables. The score is continuous at $d$, but its slope changes from zero to one. The logical statement $G_d(t)>0$ changes sharply even though the real score is continuous.

\section{Saddle-node normal form}
\begin{definition}[Field and equilibrium]
For parameter $\mu\in\mathbb R$ and state $x\in\mathbb R$, define
\[
F_\mu(x)=\mu-x^2.
\]
A state is an equilibrium when $F_\mu(x)=0$, equivalently $x^2=\mu$.
\end{definition}

The field may be viewed as the right-hand side of $\dot x=\mu-x^2$, but the classification below only requires solving its equilibrium equation.

\begin{lemma}[Negative regime]
If $\mu<0$, there is no real equilibrium.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every real square is nonnegative. Thus $x^2=\mu<0$ is impossible.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Critical regime]
At $\mu=0$, a state is an equilibrium if and only if $x=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The equation is $x^2=0$, which over the reals is equivalent to $x=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Positive branches]
If $\mu>0$, a state $x$ is an equilibrium if and only if
\[
x=\sqrt\mu\quad\text{or}\quad x=-\sqrt\mu.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Since $(\sqrt\mu)^2=\mu$, an equilibrium satisfies
\[
0=x^2-\mu=(x-\sqrt\mu)(x+\sqrt\mu).
\]
The zero-product property gives the two candidates. Conversely, each candidate squares to $\mu$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Saddle-Node Equilibrium Classification]
For $F_\mu(x)=\mu-x^2$, there are no real equilibria when $\mu<0$; when $\mu=0$, the unique equilibrium is $x=0$; and when $\mu>0$, the equilibria are exactly $x=\pm\sqrt\mu$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the preceding three lemmas and the trichotomy of real numbers.
\end{proof}

The critical equilibrium is degenerate because $\partial F_\mu/\partial x=-2x$ vanishes at $(\mu,x)=(0,0)$. The equilibrium diagram contains two curves $x=\pm\sqrt\mu$ for $\mu\ge0$, meeting at the critical point. If interpreted as an ODE, the positive branch has negative linearization and is locally attracting, whereas the negative branch has positive linearization and is locally repelling. This stability observation is contextual and is not needed for the algebraic classification.

\section{Combined transition theorem}
\begin{theorem}[Grokking--Saddle-Node Transition Theorem]
Fix any real delay $d$. The score
\[
G_d(t)=\max\{t-d,0\}
\]
fails the positivity criterion for every $t\le d$ and satisfies it for every $t>d$. Simultaneously, the field
\[
F_\mu(x)=\mu-x^2
\]
has no real equilibrium for $\mu<0$, has the unique equilibrium $x=0$ at $\mu=0$, and has exactly the equilibria $x=\pm\sqrt\mu$ for every $\mu>0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The network statement is the Delayed Generalization Theorem, and the field statement is the Saddle-Node Equilibrium Classification. Their conjunction proves the claim.
\end{proof}

The theorem asserts two exact facts without identifying $t$ with $\mu$, or $G_d(t)$ with $x$. One may align their critical coordinates through $\mu=t-d$, but that is an interpretive parameterization, not a derived law. A complete learning theory would derive parameter evolution from data and an optimizer, locate a low-dimensional center direction, and prove that its reduced dynamics have the saddle-node form with controlled error.

\section{Algorithms and numerical demonstrations}
For sample times $t_1,\dots,t_n$, delayed-score evaluation computes
\[
s_i=\max\{t_i-d,0\},\qquad g_i=(s_i>0).
\]
Each sample uses constant work, so the running time is $O(n)$. Retaining all outputs takes $O(n)$ storage; streaming needs $O(1)$ auxiliary storage. Floating-point classification close to $t=d$ is sensitive to rounding because strict positivity is threshold-sensitive. A numerical implementation should report the score and label rather than silently insert an unmotivated tolerance.

Equilibrium enumeration branches on the sign of $\mu$. It returns the empty list for $\mu<0$, the singleton $[0]$ for $\mu=0$, and $[-\sqrt\mu,\sqrt\mu]$ for $\mu>0$. Over $m$ parameters this takes $O(m)$ time and returns at most two states per parameter. Unlike generic root finding, it is exhaustive and handles criticality explicitly.

For example, let $d=3$. Times $0$, $2.9$, and $3$ all produce score zero; time $3.1$ produces $0.1$; and time $5$ produces $2$. For the field, $\mu=-1$ gives no equilibrium, $\mu=0$ gives zero, and $\mu=4$ gives $-2$ and $2$. Substitution verifies $4-(\pm2)^2=0$.

\section{Applications and conceptual consequences}
The ReLU gate exhibits a general observational effect: a latent coordinate can move smoothly while a readout remains constant on a half-line. Analogues occur in thresholded classifiers, dead-zone controllers, switching circuits, and biochemical activation. A plateau alone is therefore not evidence that the underlying system has stopped changing.

If $G_d(t)$ is a test margin, the theorem describes a delayed switch from nonpositive to positive margin. A finite test-set extension would naturally use the minimum signed margin across examples, with generalization occurring when every required margin is positive.

The saddle-node branches exhibit the scaling law $|x|=\sqrt\mu$. Thus the exact normal form has critical exponent $1/2$. Approximate square-root scaling in data can motivate a saddle-node hypothesis, but does not alone establish a causal mechanism.

Finally, continuity and logical abruptness should be separated. The real-valued score is continuous, whereas the proposition $G_d(t)>0$ changes truth value at $d$. A sudden categorical improvement can therefore arise when a continuous quantity crosses a decision threshold.

\section{Limitations and future work}
The construction is intentionally minimal. Time is supplied directly as input; no weight dynamics are derived. The network has one hidden unit and no dataset. Generalization is one scalar inequality. The delay is prescribed. Most importantly, the saddle-node field is paired with the network transition rather than derived from a neural loss landscape. The results consequently do not show that gradient descent causes grokking or that arbitrary networks undergo saddle-node bifurcations.

Natural extensions include vector-valued finite-width networks and finite test sets; derivation of the threshold trajectory from gradient flow or weight-decayed gradient descent; and an explicit train/test separation in which training error vanishes before the test margin becomes positive. Dynamical extensions should establish the usual saddle-node nondegeneracy conditions, local branch existence, and stability exchange. Robustness results should show persistence under small perturbations. The decisive connection theorem would derive the normal form from a reduced trained-network loss landscape and interpret $\mu$ as an optimizer, regularization, or data-dependent parameter.

\section{Model comparison}
The exact formulas define useful null expectations. If an observed score rises before its proposed delay, the hard-gate model requires perturbation or replacement. If inferred branches fail to approach with square-root geometry, the saddle-node form may be inappropriate locally. Agreement with one signature supports only that component; it does not establish the entire learning mechanism. This modular interpretation prevents a fit to one plot from being mistaken for a causal theory.

\section{Discussion and conclusion}
The delayed score shows exactly how visible generalization can lag behind latent motion: a nonlinear readout erases the precritical region. The saddle-node field shows exactly how a critical parameter alters the number of equilibria. Their combination offers a disciplined vocabulary for delay, threshold, degeneracy, and branching.

Observable transitions and dynamical bifurcations must nevertheless remain distinct. A score crossing is not automatically a bifurcation, and a state-space bifurcation is not automatically a theorem about test performance. A mechanistic account must provide the map between them.

For every real $d$, a width-one two-layer ReLU score can remain exactly zero through $d$ and become strictly positive at every later time. For $F_\mu(x)=\mu-x^2$, the equilibrium set changes from empty to one degenerate state and then to two square-root branches as $\mu$ crosses zero. These elementary, exact conclusions form a transparent baseline for a future dynamical theory of delayed generalization.

\end{document}

